---
import Layout from '../layouts/site.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/';
const docs = `${base}docs/`;
const commands = `pnpm add boundra zod
pnpm exec boundra init --name my-app
pnpm exec boundra create-domain order
pnpm exec boundra create-domain billing
pnpm exec boundra add-dependency billing/order
pnpm exec boundra check-boundaries`;
const tree = `domains/
  order/
    client/
    server/
    shared/
      public.ts
  billing/
    client/
    server/
    shared/`;
const contract = `import { defineQuery } from "boundra";
import { z } from "zod";

export const getOrderQuery = defineQuery({
  name: "get-order",
  input: z.object({ orderId: z.string().min(1) }),
  result: z.object({ orderId: z.string(), status: z.string() }),
});`;
const sections = [
  ['Getting started', 'Install Boundra and create your first domains.', 'user-guide/'],
  ['Boundary rules', 'Understand BR-001 through BR-006, public APIs and dependencies.', 'boundary-rules/'],
  ['Contracts', 'Define queries, mutations and routes, then connect your transport.', 'contract-schema-spec/'],
  ['Framework integration', 'Use Boundra alongside Vite or Next.js.', 'frameworks/'],
  ['Configuration', 'Configure domain discovery, paths, aliases and capabilities.', 'config-spec/'],
  ['Continuous integration', 'Run the checker before merge and require meaningful scan coverage.', 'ci-integration/'],
];
---
<Layout title="Boundra | Domain boundaries for TypeScript" description="Check TypeScript domain imports and validate application contracts with Boundra.">
  <section class="hero shell">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="version">Open source · TypeScript and Rust</p>
      <h1>Keep domain boundaries in your TypeScript codebase.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Boundra checks imports between domains and validates input and output contracts. Add it to your existing application without replacing your framework.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={`${docs}user-guide/`}>Get started <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="button secondary" href={docs}>Read documentation</a></div>
      <p class="requirements">Node.js 20+ · MIT license · CLI and runtime can be used separately</p>
    </div>
    <div class="code-panel hero-code">
      <div class="code-caption"><span>domains/billing/server/invoice.ts</span><span>TypeScript</span></div>
      <pre><code><span class="code-label">Allowed</span>{'\n'}<span class="code-keyword">import</span> {'{ getOrderQuery }'} <span class="code-keyword">from</span>{'\n'}  <span class="code-string">"@domains/order/shared/public"</span>;{'\n\n'}<span class="code-label">Disallowed</span>{'\n'}<span class="code-keyword">import</span> {'{ orderService }'} <span class="code-keyword">from</span>{'\n'}  <span class="code-string">"@domains/order/server/internal"</span>;</code></pre>
      <div class="diagnostic"><strong>BR-004</strong><span>Cross-domain internal import. Use the target domain's public API.</span></div>
      <p class="panel-note">Illustration of the boundary rule. Imports also require a declared dependency.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="shell content-section" id="quickstart">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Set up your first domains</h2><p>Start with an existing TypeScript project. Adopt Boundra one slice at a time.</p></div>
    <div class="walkthrough">
      <div><h3>1. Install and initialize</h3><p>The npm package includes a launcher for the native CLI. It downloads and verifies the matching platform binary on first use.</p><div class="code-panel"><div class="code-caption">Terminal</div><pre><code>{commands}</code></pre></div><a class="text-link" href={`${docs}cli-install/`}>Installation and supported platforms →</a></div>
      <div><h3>2. Organize domain code</h3><p>The CLI generates a manifest and layer directories. Billing can use order's public API because the dependency is declared.</p><div class="file-tree"><pre><code>{tree}</code></pre></div><a class="text-link" href={`${docs}domain-manifest-spec/`}>Domain manifest reference →</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="shell content-section split-section">
    <div><h2>Validate a contract</h2><p>Define the shape of a request and its result once. Boundra validates both ends of a call using your schemas.</p><p>Keep your existing backend and routing. Connect the runtime to a local transport or your own HTTP adapter.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${docs}user-guide/#4-implement-a-real-contract`}>Walk through a working contract →</a></div>
    <div class="code-panel"><div class="code-caption">domains/order/shared/contracts/get-order.ts</div><pre><code>{contract}</code></pre></div>
  </section>
  <section class="shell content-section split-section" id="ci">
    <div><h2>Run the checker before merge</h2><p>Add a boundary check to your CI. The CLI produces machine-readable violations and returns a nonzero exit code when checks fail.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${docs}ci-integration/`}>CI configuration and exit codes →</a></div>
    <div class="code-panel"><div class="code-caption">Terminal</div><pre><code>pnpm exec boundra check-boundaries --format json</code></pre></div>
  </section>
  <section class="shell content-section" id="documentation">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Documentation</h2><p>Read the full guides and API references on this site.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-list">{sections.map(([title, description, href]) => <a href={`${docs}${href}`}><div><h3>{title}</h3><p>{description}</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>)}</div>
    <a class="text-link" href={docs}>View all documentation →</a>
  </section>
  <section class="shell content-section fit-section"><h2>Is Boundra right for your project?</h2><p>Use it when domain APIs and dependency directions need to be explicit. It does not replace authorization, routing, persistence, a bundler, or application tests.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${docs}product-positioning/`}>Compare adoption options and limitations →</a></section>
</Layout>
